  Question 2 costing:
  (a) Halo from real bound: no bound exists (drift, σ re-estimation). Could bound span empirically per dataset: run once, measure p100 span, set halo. But span measured banded ≠ span unbanded. Adaptive fixpoint: double halo until no kept trace's extent touches halo boundary — but that only guarantees no truncation; contention effects inside halo region remain (Example B happens entirely within ±halo of edge). So NOT exact even with infinite fixpoint on span alone: exactness requires that for every trace kept in band b, the SET of peaks MTD would have assigned in the full run is present and identically claimed — contention is global (apex intensity order across the whole window). To be exact, each band must replicate the global claim order for all peaks that any of its traces could touch — the influence closure is the whole window in the worst case. Verdict: not exact, any width. Cost: reruns; verification only against N=1.
  (b) Gap partitioning: exact iff gaps wider than max influence reach (span + contention). Unbounded in theory; in practice DIA fragment m/z is dense — likely no usable gaps, forcing N=1 anyway in dense windows; also breaks load balance (gap positions ≠ equal work). Conditional-exact, verifiable per run: after tracing, check max span < min gap margin; if violated, flag. Cost: possibly no parallelism where dense; imbalance. Verification: per-run certificate (max trace extent vs cut margins) — this is actually the ONLY scheme that ships with a runtime exactness certificate: if every trace's extent is ≥3σ_max away from every cut... hmm, need margin for contention: two traces in different bands can't share a peak if cut gap > both their reaches. Certificate: no peak lies within (max per-trace extent) of a cut... Actually simpler: after the run, for each trace, compute [min_mz - 0, max_mz + 0]... the contention channel requires peaks shared between traces in different cores; if gap G between bands and every trace extent < G/2... define: cut at g (gap from g1 to g2, no peaks). Traces kept left have all members ≤ g1... no wait, halo means bands see across. In gap scheme there are no halos — a trace's peaks all lie within its band because no trace can span the gap (if gap > max span... circular). Post-hoc certificate: every detected trace's m/z extent lies entirely within its band's peak range AND no two traces from different bands have overlapping [minmember-3σ, maxmember+3σ] intervals across a cut — cheap to check. If the certificate holds, output == unbanded (argue: MTD's decisions for a trace depend only on peaks within its reach; if reaches don't cross cuts, runs are independent... but apex ORDERING is global — does cross-band apex ordering matter if influence sets are disjoint? If two traces' reachable peak sets are disjoint, their relative processing order doesn't matter — visited bitset interactions only matter for shared peaks. Noise filtering, apex gating are per-peak. So YES: disjoint influence ⇒ exact. And influence set of a trace = peaks within ±3σ_t of its running centroid path — bounded by [min member - 3σ_max, max member + 3σ_max]. So certificate: cuts placed such that no trace's inflated extent crosses → exact. That's a real exact scheme with a runtime proof, provided gaps exist.) Good — this is the strongest constructive answer. Cost: find gaps > threshold, balance work by merging gap-delimited segments into balanced bins respecting cuts (bin-packing), N becomes data-derived and thus machine-independent! Actually this also solves question 3 partially: gap-derived bands are data-dependent but NOT machine-dependent, and exact (certified), so N can vary. Nice.
  (c) Whole-window detection, parallelise differently: exact by construction (it IS the reference). Cost: trace stage serial per window, 24-way window parallelism max; measured N=4 18:25 vs N=12 12:58 — N=1 worse still (superlinear: single band holds all peaks). The superlinear cost means exactness has a real price; mitigate by overlapping window tasks (already), heaviest-first (already), and parallelising the other 51% harder. Verification: none needed — it is the reference.
  (d) Pin N: not exact vs unbanded, but deterministic and machine-independent. Cost: zero code; verification: CI digest at pinned N. The approximation error becomes a fixed property measured once (question 4). This is the pragmatic recommendation combined with reporting.

  Question 4 measurement: 
  - Reference: N=1 (bands=1 path, line 1094-1097) on representative data. 
  - Trace level: per window, match traces N=12 vs N=1 by centroid m/z within 3σ and RT overlap ≥50%; statistics: % traces unmatched each way, distribution of centroid Δmz (ppm), Δarea. 
  - Peptide level (the gate): accepted-peptide SET symmetric difference / |union| (Jaccard distance), direction counts. 
  - Statistic to report: e.g., "N=12 vs N=1: X of Y peptides differ (Jaccard d=...)". 
  - Frequency: full run costs 18 min/160 GB — run per dataset release or when trace-stage code changes, not per commit. Per-commit CI: small file + [det] digests (traceDigest_ line 2524, precursor digest line 2165) at pinned N vs stored golden — that guards determinism, not approximation error. For error: the digests make the N=1 vs N=12 comparison cheap to automate on a down-sampled input where N=1 is affordable.
  - Also zero-cost incidence instrumentation: log max trace m/z extent vs halo (mechanism 2 incidence) and count of traces whose extent crosses within 3σ of an edge (mechanism 3 exposure bound) into span_log (line 1139-1155 already logs RT spans — add m/z extents). This turns "claimed away" into "measured per run."

  Question 5: keep parallelism without partition:
  - The task pool already fills cores across windows; bands only reduce per-band peak count (superlinear cost) and lift the 24-window ceiling. Without bands: trace CPU rises superlinearly (measured 2,972→11,168 s going 24→5 bands; N=1 higher still) — so the honest price of exactness is roughly 2-4× trace CPU and the 24-core ceiling. 
  - Options within rules: (i) gap-based partition (question 2b) — keeps most parallelism, exact with certificate, machine-independent; cost: imbalance, possibly few gaps in dense windows (measure gap distribution first! The quantile comment at 1024-1025 says density varies by orders of magnitude — suggesting there ARE sparse regions; but a 141-Da-wide tile with dense peaks may have no gap > span). (ii) Accept N=1 and buy back wall time by running multiple DATASETS/windows — no. (iii) Coarser: since only 24 windows, the tail is the issue; split the LAST windows' work... still partition. 
  - Also: MTD superlinearity comes from candidate scans per apex; banding reduces candidates. An exact equivalent: precompute per-spectrum m/z-sorted structure — MTD already uses MZBegin/findNearest; the superlinearity is in the apex list × extension cost... can't touch OpenMS.
  - Verdict: exact parallelism = gap-partition with certificate if the data has gaps, else window-level only and pay. There is no free lunch: any fixed-count equipartition that reduces peak count per MTD call changes MTD's input and therefore its output.

